4. System Features

The application provides the following major features.

4.1 Add Employee

Users can add three different types of employees:

Full-Time Employee
Manager
Intern

For a full-time employee, the system accepts the basic salary. For a manager, it additionally accepts bonus and team size. For an intern, the monthly stipend is entered.

The system also checks whether the employee ID already exists before adding a new employee.

4.2 View All Employees

The system displays all stored employee records in a formatted table containing:

Employee ID
Name
Department
Employee Type
Basic Salary
Net Salary

Employees are displayed according to their employee ID.

4.3 Generate Payslip

The user can enter an employee ID to generate an individual payslip.

The payslip contains:

Employee ID
Employee name
Department
Employee type
Basic salary
Gross salary
Deductions
Net salary

Managers also have their team size and bonus displayed on their payslip.

4.4 Update Employee

Existing employee information can be updated using the employee ID.

The user can update:

Name
Department
Salary/Stipend

The system allows the user to press Enter to keep an existing value.

4.5 Delete Employee

The system allows an employee record to be deleted by entering the employee ID.

If the employee does not exist, an appropriate error message is displayed.

4.6 Search by Department

Users can search for employees belonging to a particular department.

The department comparison is case-insensitive, allowing searches such as IT, it, or It to identify the same department.

4.7 Department Payroll Summary

The system calculates the total net payroll for each department.

It also displays the total payroll of the entire company. A TreeMap is used to maintain department-wise results in sorted key order.

4.8 Sort Employees by Net Salary

Employees can be displayed in descending order of their net salary.

This allows the payroll records to be viewed from the highest net salary to the lowest.

5. Object-Oriented Programming Concepts

The project makes use of several important OOP concepts.

5.1 Abstraction

Employee is an abstract class containing common employee properties and abstract methods such as:

calculateGrossSalary()
calculateDeductions()
getType()
toCSV()

This provides a common structure for different employee types.

5.2 Inheritance

The project uses inheritance to create specialized employee classes.

The structure is:

Employee
   |
   +-- FullTimeEmployee
   |       |
   |       +-- Manager
   |
   +-- Intern

FullTimeEmployee and Intern inherit from Employee, while Manager extends FullTimeEmployee.

5.3 Polymorphism

Methods such as calculateGrossSalary(), calculateDeductions(), getType(), and toCSV() are overridden by subclasses.

This allows the payroll manager to work with different employee objects through the common Employee reference.

5.4 Encapsulation

Employee properties are accessed through getter and setter methods. For example, employee name, department, salary, manager bonus, and team size can be accessed or modified through dedicated methods.

6. Payroll Calculation

The payroll calculation differs according to the employee type.

Full-Time Employee

The system calculates:

HRA = 20% of Basic Salary

DA = 10% of Basic Salary

Therefore:

Gross Salary = Basic Salary + HRA + DA

The deductions are:

Tax = 10% of Gross Salary

PF = 1800

Therefore:

Net Salary = Gross Salary − Deductions

These calculations are implemented in the FullTimeEmployee class.

Manager

A manager follows the full-time employee salary calculation and additionally receives a bonus.

Manager Gross Salary = Full-Time Gross Salary + Bonus

The manager class also stores the team size.

Intern

For an intern:

Gross Salary = Monthly Stipend

Deductions = 0

Therefore:

Net Salary = Monthly Stipend

This calculation is implemented in the Intern class.

7. Data Storage and File Handling

The application stores employee records in:

data/employees.txt

When the application starts, the PayrollManager loads existing employee records from the file. Whenever an employee is added, updated, or deleted, the data is saved back to the file.

The employee records are stored in CSV-style format. For example, the system uses fields representing:

Employee Type, ID, Name, Department, Salary, Extra, Team Size

The program parses these records and reconstructs the appropriate employee object.

8. Exception Handling

Custom exceptions are used to handle important errors.

DuplicateEmployeeException

This exception is generated when the user attempts to add an employee with an ID that already exists.

EmployeeNotFoundException

This exception is used when an employee with the requested ID cannot be found.

The program also handles invalid numerical input, negative values, invalid salary values, and file input/output errors.

9. Class Structure

The major classes used in the project are:

Class	Responsibility
Main	Provides the command-line interface and menu
Employee	Abstract base class for employees
FullTimeEmployee	Handles full-time employee payroll
Manager	Handles manager-specific salary and bonus
Intern	Handles intern stipend
PayrollManager	Manages employee records and payroll operations
DuplicateEmployeeException	Handles duplicate employee IDs
EmployeeNotFoundException	Handles missing employee records

The PayrollManager is responsible for adding, updating, deleting, searching, sorting, calculating payroll, and handling persistent employee data.

10. Input Validation

The application includes validation to reduce incorrect user input.

Examples include:

Employee ID must be greater than zero.
Team size cannot be negative.
Salary must be positive when creating an employee.
Name and department cannot be empty.
Invalid numerical input is handled without terminating the program.
Negative salary values are rejected during updates.

These validations are implemented through reusable input methods in the Main class.

